<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #ffffff; margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="color: #ffffff; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About the Weather</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> A Perfect Day!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Describe everyday objects using visual support.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information in texts by pointing to related images during stories (e.g., points to clouds when discussing "It's cloudy."). Can describe everyday objects using visual support (e.g., points to the sun in a weather chart and says "sunny"). Can participate in weather chants by pointing to visuals (e.g., points to an umbrella during a "rainy day" song).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 60%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and initiates a "Weather Watch" activity. Teacher displays large weather flashcards (sun, rain, cloud, wind, umbrella, coat, boots). Teacher points to each flashcard and asks, "What's the weather like today?" and models "It's sunny today!" or "It's rainy." Students repeat chorally. The teacher then asks, "What do we wear on a rainy day?" pointing to an umbrella, boots, coat, and guides students to say "umbrella!" or "boots!" This reviews previous vocabulary and connects to the theme "A Perfect Day!".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models how to point to a visual and say the corresponding word or short phrase. For example, the teacher holds up a "rainy" flashcard, points to the raindrops, and says clearly, "It's rainy!" (emphasizing /r/). The teacher then models pointing to a "sunny" flashcard and saying, "It's sunny!" (emphasizing /s/). The teacher also models pointing to a picture of someone holding an umbrella and saying, "I need my umbrella." and "It's a rainy day, I play inside."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding by using simple, visual-based questions.
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a sun flashcard) "Is it rainy?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cloud flashcard) "Is it sunny?" (Students respond: "No! It's cloudy!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a picture of an umbrella) "What is this for?" (Students respond: "Rainy day!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple, colorful "Daily Weather Chart" with large icons for sun, cloud, rain, wind, and accompanying very short sentences (e.g., "Sunny day!", "Rainy day!", "Windy day!"). The chart also includes small pictures of a coat, umbrella, hat, boots. Genre: Visual information chart, Register: Informative, child-friendly, Format: Picture chart with labels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will use visual information in the weather chart to understand and convey meaning about daily weather conditions and appropriate clothing. They will point to related images during teacher's descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to an icon on the Daily Weather Chart (e.g., the sun) and says, "It's a sunny day." Students point to the same icon.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "It's windy today!" (emphasizing initial /w/ and final /i/ sounds in "windy"). Students point to the "windy" icon on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "What do I wear on a rainy day?" while pointing to the "rainy" icon. Students point to the umbrella and coat pictures on the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher then asks, "What is the weather like?" and points to a picture of clouds. Students chorally respond, "It's cloudy!"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using visual information to convey simple weather descriptions. Grammar: Adjectives + nouns (e.g., "sunny day", "rainy day", "windy day"), Present simple ("I like sunny days.", "I wear a coat."), WH-questions ("What is the weather like?"). Vocabulary: sun, rain, cloud, wind, umbrella, coat, boots, hat, day, like, wear, play, inside, outside.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Match the Weather":</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with columns of weather icons on one side (sun, cloud, rain, wind) and simple descriptive sentences on the other (e.g., "It is a sunny day.", "It is a rainy day. I need my umbrella.", "It is a windy day. I wear a hat."). Students draw lines to match the weather icon to the correct sentence. Teacher walks around, providing support and checking for accuracy, specifically focusing on students connecting the visual information to the written text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Complete the Sentence":</b> The teacher holds up weather flashcards one by one. For each card, students complete a sentence orally.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows sun) "It's a _______ day. I like _______ days." (Students respond: "sunny," "sunny")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows rain) "It's a _______ day. I need my _______." (Students respond: "rainy," "umbrella")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher shows wind) "It's a _______ day. I play _______." (Students respond: "windy," "outside")</li>
                        </ul>
                        The teacher encourages full sentences and models pronunciation of /s/, /r/, /w/, /i/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Tell Your Partner":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small weather flashcards. Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and says, "It's a _______ day." Student B responds by saying an activity or item of clothing, e.g., "I play outside." or "I need my boots." They then switch roles. The focus is on using the visual to initiate a simple description and response. The teacher circulates, observing and assisting, ensuring students mediate the visual information into verbal output.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About the Weather".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Perfect Day Weather Journal" Presentation – Students will present one page from their previously started "Weather Journal" (Project 1: Weather Journal). They will use their drawings as visual support to describe the weather and an activity. This is a mini-presentation to a small group or the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Prepare Your Journal:</b> Students open their "Weather Journal" to the page they want to present. The teacher reminds them to look at their drawing and think about the weather and what they like to do on that day.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Group Presentation:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each student takes a turn presenting one journal page. They point to their drawing (e.g., a sun and a child playing) and say: "This is a sunny day. I like sunny days, and I play outside." or "This is a rainy day. I wear my coat and boots." (Pragmatic: Describing the weather and activities, using 'and' as discourse marker; Sociolinguistic: Expressing preferences).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Interaction:</b> Group members listen and can ask simple questions like "Is it sunny?" (pointing to the drawing) or "Do you like rainy days?" (Mediation in this stage is key, as students are conveying the meaning of their visual creation through spoken language).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering prompts and encouragement, ensuring students are using their visuals to mediate their message.</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation using a simple checklist, focusing on students' ability to mediate visual information verbally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student individually presents one page from their "Weather Journal" to the teacher or a small peer group. They must point to their drawing and describe the weather and an activity using at least two simple sentences, effectively mediating the meaning of their visual.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mediation:</b> Accurately points to visuals to convey meaning (e.g., points to sun when saying "sunny").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 2-3 target vocabulary words (e.g., "sunny," "rainy," "play," "umbrella," "coat").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Attempts to use Adjectives + nouns ("sunny day") and Present Simple ("I like," "I play").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Generally intelligible, with an attempt at /s/ in "sunny" and /r/ in "rainy".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity:</b> Message is understandable with visual support.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Effort/Engagement:</b> Shows effort in communicating and participating.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face if they could explain their picture, a neutral face if they needed help, or a sad face if it was difficult. They also answer (orally or by pointing to a word/picture):
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I point to my picture when I talked?" (Yes/No gesture)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'sunny day' and 'rainy day'?" (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite picture to draw in your Weather Journal?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you say about your favorite weather?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in integrating the "Weather Journal" project with the mediation skill? Did students successfully use their drawings to convey meaning? Were the Pre-A1.4 level tasks appropriate for visual-to-verbal mediation? What adjustments are needed, especially for students who struggled to connect visuals with language? Perhaps more explicit sentence frames or flashcard practice for those needing more support. This being the final lesson of the theme, it provided a good consolidation of vocabulary and structures.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "All About the Weather" theme and the "Weather Journal" project. The next sequence of lessons will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on students' foundational skills of linking visuals to language and simple description.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>